    "input": "Each seed's archived train stream_08, 1024 ticks x 32 actors x 2 jobs. Only obs enters load_native(seed).train(); h/z/M start at zero as in CE training.",
    "execution": "Three independent CPU processes, one intra-op and one inter-op thread each. FP32 forward/backward, TF32 disabled. Float64 used only for norms/dots of already-computed gradient vectors.",
    "mode_definitions": {
      "WAIT": "action == 0",
      "BEGIN": "action in {1,2}",
      "FINISH": "action in {3,...,10}"
    },
    "mean_mode_loss": "For each mode, mean ordinary cross-entropy over the original 11 native action logits and the actual teacher action labels in that mode.",
    "original_weighted_ce": "sum_i w_i CE_i / sum_i w_i, with w_WAIT=.25 and all other class weights=1.",
    "mode_coefficient": "w_mode * n_mode / sum_i w_i; multiplying the mode mean loss/gradient gives its exact original weighted-CE contribution.",
    "gradient_cosine": "Cosine between flattened gradients of mean mode losses over all 8210 trainable parameters.",
    "proposed_equal_mode_ce": "(mean_CE_WAIT + mean_CE_BEGIN + mean_CE_FINISH) / 3",
    "wait_projection_relative_to_finish_component": "(alpha_WAIT * ||g_WAIT|| * cosine(g_WAIT,g_FINISH)) / (alpha_FINISH * ||g_FINISH||). Negative means that WAIT opposes the FINISH-axis component; it is not a measured change in training outcomes.",
    "combined_gradient_dot_interpretation": "For sufficiently small gradient descent steps, a positive dot with a mode gradient predicts a decrease of that mode's loss locally, before optimizer preconditioning.",
    "limitations": [
      "One fixed archived training stream per seed, measured only at final CE endpoints.",
      "Gradient alignment is local and does not reconstruct earlier optimization or establish why binding failed.",
      "Adam, clipping, trajectory changes and generalization can make actual retraining behave differently from these raw-gradient comparisons.",
      "Three-mode balancing also changes relative gradient scale; equal update count alone does not isolate a single causal mechanism."
    ]
